      "text": "To solve for the length of the hypotenuse \\( h \\) of the right triangle, we start by identifying the lengths of the legs. We are given:\n\n\\[\n\\text{leg 1} = \\log_4 27\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{leg 2} = \\log_2 9\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the hypotenuse \\( h \\). Given that \\( h = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2} \\), we'll first convert the logarithms to a common base for easier calculation. Recall that \\(\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}\\) and \\(\\log_2 9 = \\log_2 (3^2) = 2 \\log_2 3\\).\n\nLet's compute these values:\n\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = 2 \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nNow, compute the squares:\n\n\\[\n(\\log_4 27)^2 = \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{(\\log_2 27)^2}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\log_2 9)^2 = (2 \\log_2 3)^2 = 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\nAdding these squares together:\n\n\\[\n(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2 = \\frac{(\\log_2 27)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\nRecall \\(\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3\\), so \\((\\log_2 27)^2 = 9 (\\log_2 3)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\((\\log_2 3)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n= \\left( \\frac{9}{4} + 4 \\right) (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\left( \\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{16}{4} \\right) (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{25}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nWe need to compute \\(4^h\\):\n\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3} = (2^2)^{\\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3} = 2^{5 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^5 = 3^5 = 243\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}\n\\]",
