      "text": "To solve for the hypotenuse \\( h \\) of the right triangle, we start by expressing the lengths of the legs in terms of logarithms:\n\\[\n\\text{Leg 1} = \\log_4 27 = \\log_4 (3^3) = 3 \\log_4 3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Leg 2} = \\log_2 9 = \\log_2 (3^2) = 2 \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nUsing the change of base formula for logarithms, we can rewrite \\(\\log_4 3\\) in terms of base 2:\n\\[\n\\log_4 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\nThus, the lengths of the legs can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n\\text{Leg 1} = 3 \\log_4 3 = 3 \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Leg 2} = 2 \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the hypotenuse \\( h \\):\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + (2 \\log_2 3)^2}\n\\]\n\nWe simplify inside the square root:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\log_2 3)^2 = 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2 = \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + \\frac{16 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} = \\frac{25 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\frac{25 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}} = \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\( 4^h \\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of exponents \\( a^{bc} = (a^b)^c \\):\n\\[\n4^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = \\left( (2^2) \\right)^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = 2^{2 \\cdot \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = 2^{5 \\log_2 3}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property \\( a^{\\log_b c} = c^{\\log_b a} \\):\n\\[\n2^{5 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^5 = 3^5\n\\]\n\nFinally, we compute \\( 3^5 \\):\n\\[\n3^5 = 243\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( 4^h \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}\n\\]",
