      "text": "By the Pythagorean Theorem, we have \\[\nh^2 = (\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2.\n\\] Using the change of base formula, we can rewrite this as \\[\nh^2 = \\left(\\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\log_2 9}{\\log_2 2}\\right)^2.\n\\] Simplifying, we get \\[\nh^2 = \\left(\\frac{3\\log_2 3}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{2\\log_2 3}{1}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{3\\log_2 3}{2}\\right)^2 + (2\\log_2 3)^2.\n\\] Expanding, we obtain \\[\nh^2 = \\frac{9(\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4(\\log_2 3)^2 = \\frac{25(\\log_2 3)^2}{4}.\n\\] Taking the square root, we get \\[\nh = \\frac{5\\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\] Thus, \\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5\\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{5\\log_2 3}{2}} = 2^{5\\log_2 3} = 2^{\\log_2 3^5} = 2^{\\log_2 243} = \\boxed{243}.\n\\] The answer is: 243",
