      "text": "To solve the given problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the lengths of the legs:**\n   The lengths of the legs of the right triangle are given as \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\).\n\n2. **Convert the logarithmic expressions to a common base:**\n   - First, convert \\(\\log_4 27\\) to a base 2 logarithm:\n     \\[\n     \\log_4 27 = \\log_{2^2} 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 2^2} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}\n     \\]\n     So, \\(\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}\\).\n\n   - Next, convert \\(\\log_2 9\\) to a base 4 logarithm:\n     \\[\n     \\log_2 9 = \\log_2 (3^2) = 2 \\log_2 3\n     \\]\n     We need to express \\(\\log_2 3\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms:\n     \\[\n     \\log_2 3 = \\log_4 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2}\n     \\]\n     Therefore,\n     \\[\n     \\log_2 9 = 2 \\log_4 3 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2} = \\log_2 3\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the hypotenuse \\(h\\):**\n   The hypotenuse \\(h\\) of the right triangle can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2}\n   \\]\n   Substitute the converted values:\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}\\right)^2 + (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\frac{(\\log_2 27)^2}{4} + (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3\\):\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\frac{(3 \\log_2 3)^2}{4} + (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   Factor out \\((\\log_2 3)^2\\):\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{9}{4} + 1\\right) (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{4}{4}\\right) (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\cdot \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   h = \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n\n4. **Compute \\(4^h\\):**\n   Substitute \\(h = \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\log_2 3\\) into \\(4^h\\):\n   \\[\n   4^h = 4^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\log_2 3}\n   \\]\n   Using the property of exponents \\(a^{bc} = (a^b)^c\\):\n   \\[\n   4^h = \\left(4^{\\log_2 3}\\right)^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}}\n   \\]\n   Simplify \\(4^{\\log_2 3}\\):\n   \\[\n   4^{\\log_2 3} = (2^2)^{\\log_2 3} = 2^{2 \\log_2 3} = 2^{\\log_2 9} = 9\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   4^h = 9^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}}}\n\\]",
